# Unprivileged access to the keyboard, on Linux.
#
# The kernel creates the /dev/hidraw* nodes as 0600 root:root. Without this
# rule, Candeo only sees the keyboard when started as root — which a desktop
# application has no reason to require.
#
# `TAG+="uaccess"` hands the permission over to systemd-logind: it sets an ACL
# on the node for the user of the active local session, and removes it at
# logout. That is more precise than a fixed group (`plugdev`), which would also
# give access to a remote session and to accounts that are not sitting at the
# machine.
#
# The 60 prefix is not decorative. The rule that actually applies the ACL is
# `70-uaccess.rules`, shipped by systemd; a rule that set the tag after it
# would have no effect. Any number below 70 will do.
#
# The filter is on the vendor ID alone (0x1532, Razer): the PID changes from
# one model to the next, and it is Candeo that then picks its device by VID /
# PID / interface. `ATTRS{}` — plural — walks up the chain of parents to the
# USB device, the only place where `idVendor` exists; `ATTR{}` on the hidraw
# node would not find it.
#
# The package installs this file in /usr/lib/udev/rules.d/, reserved for
# vendors. /etc/udev/rules.d/ belongs to the administrator: that is where they
# put a competing rule to overrule us, and that is where this file has to be
# copied by hand when Candeo is run from source.
SUBSYSTEM=="hidraw", ATTRS{idVendor}=="1532", MODE="0660", TAG+="uaccess"
